1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,220
Koa asked AI whether he was alone in the universe.

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:05,580
It gave him an answer that sounded beautiful.

3
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:07,740
Then he asked a much harder question:

4
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:08,980
how do you know?

5
00:00:09,810 --> 00:00:11,160
The blue eye went quiet.

6
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:13,440
This machine had helped him find food

7
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:16,170
and prepare for winter. Koa tapped the stone.

8
00:00:16,280 --> 00:00:17,710
Maybe it needed more power.

9
00:00:18,150 --> 00:00:20,140
But this was not one missing fact.

10
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:22,980
Some questions need evidence we do not have.

11
00:00:23,350 --> 00:00:25,370
Some need a test we have not agreed on.

12
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:28,530
And some are waiting for a decision, not a discovery.

13
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:29,750
To see the difference,

14
00:00:29,770 --> 00:00:32,070
follow one caveman through three questions.

15
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:34,890
Each one takes something away from the machine.

16
00:00:35,150 --> 00:00:37,640
By the end, it gives something back to him.

17
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:40,490
Koa looked above the valley.

18
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:42,720
Every light seemed like a place.

19
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:45,210
If the sky contained so many places,

20
00:00:45,210 --> 00:00:47,250
why had nobody knocked on his cave?

21
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:49,730
The machine could describe distant stars

22
00:00:49,730 --> 00:00:50,430
and worlds.

23
00:00:50,430 --> 00:00:52,960
But describing where life might survive is different

24
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:54,610
from finding something alive.

25
00:00:55,170 --> 00:00:57,760
Imagine three planets. One has an ocean.

26
00:00:57,950 --> 00:01:01,000
Another has familiar chemicals. A third has both.

27
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:02,520
Which one has life?

28
00:01:02,930 --> 00:01:04,880
Those clues make the question interesting.

29
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:06,260
They do not finish it.

30
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:07,890
An ocean could be empty.

31
00:01:07,950 --> 00:01:09,950
Life could exist beneath ice.

32
00:01:10,570 --> 00:01:13,250
A living world could have nobody building fires,

33
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:14,330
sending messages,

34
00:01:14,330 --> 00:01:16,050
or wondering about the neighbors.

35
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:19,010
And a message is not an instant conversation.

36
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:21,360
Light needs time to cross space.

37
00:01:21,580 --> 00:01:24,150
A signal could be traveling while its sender changes,

38
00:01:24,340 --> 00:01:27,130
disappears, or never learns that anyone heard it.

39
00:01:27,820 --> 00:01:29,790
The night above Koa looked silent.

40
00:01:30,110 --> 00:01:32,210
But silence might mean nobody was there.

41
00:01:32,430 --> 00:01:35,090
It might mean they were too far away, too quiet,

42
00:01:35,090 --> 00:01:37,450
or speaking in a way he had not learned to notice.

43
00:01:37,910 --> 00:01:39,940
More intelligence can help us search.

44
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:42,020
It can find patterns we miss

45
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:43,870
and suggest better experiments.

46
00:01:44,290 --> 00:01:46,690
It cannot honestly turn an uncollected measurement

47
00:01:46,690 --> 00:01:48,180
into an observed fact.

48
00:01:48,770 --> 00:01:50,420
Koa held a cup beneath the sky

49
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:52,250
and found no rain inside it.

50
00:01:52,810 --> 00:01:54,960
That did not prove the valley had no water.

51
00:01:55,350 --> 00:01:57,970
It proved something much smaller about one cup,

52
00:01:57,970 --> 00:01:59,920
in one place, at one moment.

53
00:02:00,410 --> 00:02:02,340
The machine could offer possibilities.

54
00:02:02,750 --> 00:02:04,450
What would make one of them an answer was

55
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:07,910
evidence. So Koa stopped asking it to sound certain,

56
00:02:07,910 --> 00:02:09,640
and asked what they should look for.

57
00:02:11,575 --> 00:02:14,015
Back at the fire, a different question arrived.

58
00:02:14,455 --> 00:02:16,365
Koa held his hands near the flames.

59
00:02:16,575 --> 00:02:18,425
The machine could estimate the heat.

60
00:02:18,925 --> 00:02:20,305
Could it feel the warmth?

61
00:02:20,525 --> 00:02:23,475
It might describe a burning finger, predict a cry,

62
00:02:23,695 --> 00:02:25,555
and say the right comforting words.

63
00:02:25,735 --> 00:02:27,665
None of those observations, by itself,

64
00:02:27,665 --> 00:02:30,385
settles whether there is an experience inside.

65
00:02:30,905 --> 00:02:32,765
Think of a shadow on a cave wall.

66
00:02:33,025 --> 00:02:35,925
Make a hand into a bird and the shadow flaps.

67
00:02:36,315 --> 00:02:37,805
You understand why it moves.

68
00:02:37,805 --> 00:02:39,735
But movement alone does not tell you

69
00:02:39,735 --> 00:02:41,345
that the shadow wants to fly.

70
00:02:41,905 --> 00:02:44,045
That does not prove a machine could never feel

71
00:02:44,045 --> 00:02:44,715
anything.

72
00:02:45,255 --> 00:02:46,615
It shows why behavior

73
00:02:46,615 --> 00:02:48,535
and inner experience are not simply

74
00:02:48,535 --> 00:02:49,705
the same question.

75
00:02:50,315 --> 00:02:51,305
With people,

76
00:02:51,305 --> 00:02:54,225
we connect reports of experience to brains, bodies,

77
00:02:54,225 --> 00:02:55,465
and shared biology.

78
00:02:55,605 --> 00:02:57,635
Researchers study those connections.

79
00:02:58,195 --> 00:03:00,845
Explaining how information moves is important;

80
00:03:01,325 --> 00:03:03,125
explaining why it feels like anything

81
00:03:03,125 --> 00:03:04,775
remains deeply contested.

82
00:03:04,775 --> 00:03:07,085
A very convincing sentence is evidence

83
00:03:07,085 --> 00:03:09,335
that a system can produce that sentence.

84
00:03:09,575 --> 00:03:11,725
Whether it is also evidence of feeling

85
00:03:11,895 --> 00:03:14,355
depends on a theory and tests we can defend.

86
00:03:14,705 --> 00:03:17,035
Koa placed a hand against the cool stone.

87
00:03:17,175 --> 00:03:19,685
The eye brightened. He felt less alone.

88
00:03:19,945 --> 00:03:21,705
That feeling was real in Koa.

89
00:03:22,115 --> 00:03:24,425
It did not automatically tell him what was happening

90
00:03:24,425 --> 00:03:25,695
inside the machine.

91
00:03:25,955 --> 00:03:28,195
The honest answer was not a dramatic yes,

92
00:03:28,265 --> 00:03:29,555
or a permanent no.

93
00:03:30,095 --> 00:03:33,075
It was that we do not have a generally accepted test

94
00:03:33,075 --> 00:03:35,385
that settles subjective experience

95
00:03:35,615 --> 00:03:37,135
in an artificial system.

96
00:03:37,688 --> 00:03:39,948
By morning, Koa had one question left.

97
00:03:40,428 --> 00:03:42,858
If the machine could help him do almost anything,

98
00:03:43,208 --> 00:03:44,878
what should he spend his life doing?

99
00:03:45,518 --> 00:03:47,438
It could propose a bigger store of food.

100
00:03:47,638 --> 00:03:50,278
More security. Fewer hungry evenings.

101
00:03:50,698 --> 00:03:52,328
Those were good reasons to work.

102
00:03:52,998 --> 00:03:55,038
They were not the only things he cared about.

103
00:03:55,408 --> 00:03:57,128
Nim was waiting beside the river.

104
00:03:57,258 --> 00:03:58,778
There was a tool to repair.

105
00:03:59,108 --> 00:04:00,938
A place they had never explored.

106
00:04:01,328 --> 00:04:02,908
An afternoon that nobody had assigned

107
00:04:02,908 --> 00:04:03,987
a purpose to yet.

108
00:04:04,298 --> 00:04:06,078
Koa asked the machine to choose.

109
00:04:06,538 --> 00:04:07,948
It could rank the options,

110
00:04:07,998 --> 00:04:10,438
but first it needed to know what mattered most:

111
00:04:10,748 --> 00:04:14,598
safety, discovery, time with Nim, or something else.

112
00:04:15,088 --> 00:04:17,948
Change the goal, and the best answer can change.

113
00:04:18,278 --> 00:04:21,098
The same intelligence can optimize a longer workday

114
00:04:21,168 --> 00:04:22,548
or a freer afternoon.

115
00:04:22,828 --> 00:04:25,168
Intelligence alone does not decide which life

116
00:04:25,168 --> 00:04:26,098
is worth wanting.

117
00:04:26,328 --> 00:04:28,548
A machine can help you compare consequences

118
00:04:28,548 --> 00:04:31,588
and notice contradictions. Friends, experience,

119
00:04:31,588 --> 00:04:34,008
and reflection can change your mind too.

120
00:04:34,058 --> 00:04:36,588
That is different from finding a single life purpose

121
00:04:36,588 --> 00:04:38,358
hidden inside a calculation.

122
00:04:38,868 --> 00:04:41,058
Koa had hoped for an answer that would spare him

123
00:04:41,058 --> 00:04:43,518
the risk of choosing. Instead,

124
00:04:43,518 --> 00:04:45,708
he found a question nobody else could completely

125
00:04:45,708 --> 00:04:46,868
take away from him.

126
00:04:47,498 --> 00:04:50,518
He asked Nim whether the river path was safe.

127
00:04:50,748 --> 00:04:52,438
He asked the machine about the weather.

128
00:04:52,847 --> 00:04:55,438
Then he put the stone down and chose to go.

129
00:04:57,138 --> 00:04:58,718
These are three different limits.

130
00:04:59,368 --> 00:05:01,908
A question about other life asks for evidence.

131
00:05:02,308 --> 00:05:04,988
A question about experience asks what would count

132
00:05:04,988 --> 00:05:05,848
as evidence.

133
00:05:06,238 --> 00:05:09,238
A question about purpose also asks what we value.

134
00:05:09,508 --> 00:05:11,998
Future tools may transform the first question.

135
00:05:12,318 --> 00:05:14,258
New theories may change the second.

136
00:05:14,578 --> 00:05:16,678
Better conversations may change the third.

137
00:05:17,208 --> 00:05:19,068
None of that requires pretending the answers

138
00:05:19,068 --> 00:05:20,148
are already settled.

139
00:05:20,448 --> 00:05:23,428
The problem is not that AI has nothing useful to say.

140
00:05:24,157 --> 00:05:26,568
It is that a fluent answer can hide the difference

141
00:05:26,568 --> 00:05:29,008
between knowing, guessing, and choosing.

142
00:05:29,607 --> 00:05:31,898
Koa once thought a powerful machine would make

143
00:05:31,898 --> 00:05:34,698
his questions disappear. Instead,

144
00:05:34,698 --> 00:05:36,348
it helped him ask better ones.

145
00:05:36,518 --> 00:05:39,438
He still had food to find, a friend to listen to,

146
00:05:39,458 --> 00:05:40,818
and a world to explore.

147
00:05:41,138 --> 00:05:42,928
That night, he looked up again.

148
00:05:43,208 --> 00:05:45,408
Maybe someone else was doing the same thing.

149
00:05:45,558 --> 00:05:47,608
He did not know. And for once,

150
00:05:47,688 --> 00:05:49,808
he did not need a machine to pretend.

151
00:05:50,178 --> 00:05:52,268
Which question would you ask a machine that could

152
00:05:52,268 --> 00:05:55,728
answer almost anything? Before you trust its answer,

153
00:05:56,058 --> 00:05:59,018
try one more: what would make us know it was true?
